{"summary":"UNVERIFIED MODEL KNOWLEDGE — no sources were supplied, so nothing below has been checked against current legislation or guidance; citations are deliberately empty. The premise of the question is partly mistaken. Pension Credit has no fixed upper capital or savings limit that automatically disqualifies a claimant. Instead, capital is converted into a notional (\"deemed\") weekly income under the State Pension Credit Regulations: a lower tranche of capital is disregarded entirely, and each part-tranche above that threshold is treated as producing a set weekly amount, which is then added to other income when comparing income with the appropriate minimum guarantee. On my (unverified) recollection the disregard has long been £10,000, with £1 per week assumed for each £500 or part of £500 above it. High capital therefore reduces or extinguishes entitlement only indirectly, by pushing assessed income above the relevant threshold — the point at which that happens depends on household circumstances, which I have been instructed not to assume or compute. Distinguish the whole benefit (Guarantee Credit, and Savings Credit for those with a qualifying pre-April-2016 protected status) from additional amounts for severe disability, carers, certain housing costs and dependent children. Note also that £16,000-style capital cut-offs belong to other benefits (e.g. Housing Benefit, Universal Credit), not to Pension Credit itself."}
,"claims":[{"statement":"There is no maximum savings or capital figure above which Pension Credit is automatically refused.","qualification":"Unverified model knowledge, not checked against current regulations; capital instead affects entitlement through a deemed-income (tariff income) calculation, so very large holdings can still end an award in practice.","citations":[]},{"statement":"Capital below a disregard threshold — to my recollection £10,000 — is ignored, and capital above it is treated as generating a fixed weekly income for each £500 or part thereof.","qualification":"Unverified; the threshold and tariff rate are set by regulation and may have changed, and different rules may apply to care-home residents; I cannot confirm the figures currently in force.","citations":[]},{"statement":"Whether assessed income (including deemed income from capital) defeats a claim depends on the appropriate minimum guarantee for the household, including any additional amounts.","qualification":"Unverified; additional amounts for severe disability, carers, housing costs or children are conditions of a higher threshold, not separate benefits, and I am not assessing any individual's entitlement.","citations":[]},{"statement":"The commonly cited £16,000 capital limit is a feature of other means-tested benefits, not of Pension Credit.","qualification":"Unverified; receipt of Guarantee Credit may itself passport a claimant past capital tests in some of those other schemes, but that is a separate legal question.","citations":[]}]
